      "excerpts": [
        {
          "line": 1,
          "text": "# Example: Using a2a-python SDK Without an LLM Framework"
        },
        {
          "line": 3,
          "text": "This repository demonstrates how to set up and use the [a2a-python SDK](https://github.com/google/a2a-python) to create a simple server and client, without relying on any agent framework."
        },
        {
          "line": 7,
          "text": "- **A2A (Agent-to-Agent):** A protocol and SDK for communication with AI Agents."
        },
        {
          "line": 14,
          "text": "- An API key for Gemini (set as `GEMINI_API_KEY`)"
        },
        {
          "line": 34,
          "text": "   export GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key"
        },
        {
          "line": 40,
          "text": "   GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key"
        },
        {
          "line": 48,
          "text": "uv run --env-file .env python -m src.no_llm_framework.server.__main__"
        },
        {
          "line": 58,
          "text": "uv run --env-file .env python -m src.no_llm_framework.client --question \"What is A2A protocol?\""
        },
        {
          "line": 69,
          "text": "- `src/no_llm_framework/server/`: Server implementation."
        },
        {
          "line": 70,
          "text": "- `src/no_llm_framework/client/`: Client implementation."
        },
        {
          "line": 76,
          "text": "- **API key errors:** Ensure `GEMINI_API_KEY` is set correctly."
        },
        {
          "line": 81,
          "text": "Important: The sample code provided is for demonstration purposes and illustrates the mechanics of the Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol. When building production applications, it is critical to treat any agent operating outside of your direct control as a potentially untrusted entity."
        },
        {
          "line": 83,
          "text": "All data received from an external agent\u2014including but not limited to its AgentCard, messages, artifacts, and task statuses\u2014should be handled as untrusted input. For example, a malicious agent could provide an AgentCard containing crafted data in its fields (e.g., description, name, skills.description). If this data is used without sanitization to construct prompts for a Large Language Model (LLM), it could expose your application to prompt injection attacks.  Failure to properly validate and sanitize this data before use can introduce security vulnerabilities into your application."
        }
      ],
      "completed_at_utc": "2026-10-23T22:08:35.072121+00:00"
    },
